/*
DESCRIPTION 

Now that the reference points have been sampled, we need to create probability layers
that will eventually feed into our assemblage logic. In order to do that we will be
using random forest classifier with output mode set as probability. Since the classification
will be probability of a certain biophysical feature existing within that pixel, we will use
perform classification using only two classes 1. class that specifies that a certain feature
exists 2. class that specifies that said feature does not exist. For example, if we want to
generate a tree cover primitive, we will set points that symbolize tree cover as class 1 and
other points such as built up, rivers, etc as class 0.
 
SCRIPT INFOS
 
The variables that users can/should change are
1. boundary - this should contain the boundary of region of interest
2. repository - this is the image collection that contains the composites
3. primitive - it is the name of the primitive (as in reference data) which we want to prepare
    primitive layer for
4. year - this is the year for which we want to produce the primitive layer
5. sampledTableId - this should contain the asset Id or fusion table id (starting with a "ft:"
    if its a fusion table) for the sampled table
6. landClassField - this should contain the name of column that contains the
    land class information
7. exportScale - this is the spatial resolution of the export layer
8. numberOfTrees - (optional) the number of trees used by random forest

The users need to do the following  
1. After the script is run, initiate the export tasks by going to the "Tasks" tab ------->>>>>>>>>>
    Note that there will be multiple exports for each year

DEVELOPER NOTES

1. Note that the feature importance in the script containing feature importances need to be
    updated based on your sampled data. We do this by running R script that evaluates the 
    feature importance of the classifier model. 
2. If you do not wish to run feature importance, you can just comment the line 105 (not 
    recommended) to ommit the select part.
*/

